Silt-Furnace

Ingredients: PROGRESSIVE METAL + AFROBEAT + COZY, WARM & CHILL LO-FI + Synth Brass  
Creative Catalyst: Born at an illegal rooftop rave over the brackish tide of a half-submerged Lagos-on-Thames, 2091.

A paradoxical collision where polyrhythmic, hyper-syncopated Afrobeat grooves and drop-tuned eight-string guitar djent are crushed through saturated cassette tape heads, smothered in vinyl crackle, and lifted toward dawn by triumphant, honey-soaked analog synth horn fanfares.

Genre DNA

*   Tempo & Pulse: 98–112 BPM. A buoyant, swung Afrobeat pocket overlaid with polymetric 11/8 and 7/8 djent cross-rhythms; the kick lazily sits behind the grid while the syncopated hi-hats flutter with organic tape flutter.
*   Harmonic Palette: Lush Dorian and Mixolydian ♭6 modal tapestries colliding with jagged, diminished math-metal arpeggios, tempered by pillowy, tape-warped major 9th chords.
*   Production Signatures: 
    1. Submerged Tube Saturation: 8-string baritone guitars run through boutique tube heads directly into rain-damaged cassette decks for a velvety, fuzz-fur edge without harsh top-end bite.
    2. Resin-Smoked Brass Layering: Monophonic CS-80 and Prophet synth brass patches run through analog bucket-brigade delays to sound like melted, distant steam whistles.
    3. Brine-Room Microphoning: Live shekere, talking drums, and water-filled glass carboys tracked with dusty ribbon microphones picking up the natural slapback of flooded urban canyons.
*   Dynamics: Long, cozy passages of sleepy electric piano, vinyl rain, and rubbery bass vamps that unexpectedly detonate into crushing, complex polyrhythmic breakdowns without ever losing their warm, herbal, fireside intimacy.

Scene & Origin Lore

In the monsoon summer of 2091, the salt tides claimed the lower sixty floors of the Old Financial Sector, forcing the diaspora into the sky. On the corrugated-iron heli-deck of Tower 4, unlicensed sound system collectives dragged up solar generators, water-damaged Oberheim synthesizers, rusted baritone guitars, and traditional dundun drums. Dressed in salt-stained oilskin parkas lined with faux-alpaca fur, patchwork batik boiler suits, and copper-tinted diver goggles, this roof-bound counterculture concocted Silt-Furnace. They brewed fermented hibiscus tea over brazier fires while dancing in hypnotic, foot-dragging steps to math-metal grooves tempered by the sun-drenched swing of West African brass. It was metal for sleepwalkers and Afrobeat for deep-sea divers—a defiance of catastrophe wrapped in an heirloom wool blanket.

Typical Instruments: Drop-E 8-String Meshuggah-spec Guitars, Talking Drum (Dundun), Shekere, Yamaha CS-80 Synth Brass, Crumar Orchestrator, Dusty Rhodes 73 Piano, Micro-tuned Kalimba, SP-404 Sampler, 5-String Fretless Bass.

